# 07 -- The "named" layer:  let, let rec, and why a name is only sugar
#
# 'let' is ordinary substitution and 'let rec' is a name for the Y
# combinator.  Running this file (and watching the trace) is the clearest
# way to see that names add no power -- they are just a convenient notation
# on top of the pure lambda calculus.
# Try:  python lambda_calculus.py examples/07_letrec.lc
#       python lambda_calculus.py examples/07_letrec.lc --expand   # pure beta

# Plain let: pure substitution.
run let twice = \f. \x. f (f x) in twice (\y. SUCC y)

# let with several parameters:
run let add3 = \a. \b. \c. ADD a (ADD b c) in add3 ONE TWO THREE

# let rec: the name may appear in its own body.  It desugars to Y.
run let rec fact = \n. IF (IS_ZERO n) ONE (MULT n (fact (PRED n))) in fact THREE

# ... and with parameter sugar, exactly like a normal function definition:
run let rec fact n = IF (IS_ZERO n) ONE (MULT n (fact (PRED n))) in fact THREE
